/**
  @page guides-mpi MPI: Distributed Computing
  @brief Guide to distributed-memory parallel computing with Rodin's MPI module

  @tableofcontents

  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-mpi-intro Introduction

  Rodin's MPI module enables distributed finite element computations across
  multiple processes using the Message Passing Interface.  It builds on
  [Boost.MPI](https://www.boost.org/doc/libs/release/doc/html/mpi.html) and
  provides:

  - A @ref Rodin::Context::MPI "Context::MPI" execution context wrapping a
    Boost.MPI environment and communicator.
  - A @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh<Context::MPI> "Mesh\<Context::MPI\>"
    distributed mesh that stores a rank-local shard with ownership and ghost
    metadata.
  - A @ref Rodin::Geometry::Sharder<Context::MPI> "Sharder" that partitions a
    global mesh and distributes shards to MPI ranks.
  - A `reconcile()` method that resolves shared entity ownership and
    distributed indices after local connectivity discovery.

  Include the module via:
  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/MPI.h>
  @endcode

  @section guides-mpi-context Execution Context

  The @ref Rodin::Context::MPI "Context::MPI" class wraps a
  `boost::mpi::environment` and `boost::mpi::communicator`:

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <boost/mpi/environment.hpp>
  #include <boost/mpi/communicator.hpp>
  #include <Rodin/MPI.h>

  namespace mpi = boost::mpi;

  int main(int argc, char** argv)
  {
    mpi::environment env(argc, argv);
    mpi::communicator world;
    Rodin::Context::MPI mpi(env, world);

    // mpi.getCommunicator() -> const boost::mpi::communicator&
    // mpi.getEnvironment()  -> const boost::mpi::environment&
  }
  @endcode

  The context is passed to distributed mesh constructors and sharders.  It
  does not own the Boost.MPI objects — they must outlive the context.

  @section guides-mpi-mesh Distributed Mesh

  @ref Rodin::Geometry::Mesh<Context::MPI> "Mesh\<Context::MPI\>" (aliased as
  `Geometry::MPIMesh`) stores a rank-local
  @ref Rodin::Geometry::Shard "Shard" together with an MPI context.  Geometric
  queries that require global information (polytope counts, volume, perimeter)
  perform MPI reductions automatically.

  @subsection guides-mpi-mesh-uniformgrid UniformGrid

  The simplest way to create a distributed mesh is `UniformGrid`, which
  partitions and distributes internally:

  @code{.cpp}
  auto mesh = Mesh<Context::MPI>::UniformGrid(
      mpi, Polytope::Type::Tetrahedron, {16, 16, 16});
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-mpi-mesh-sharder Explicit Partitioning with Sharder

  For more control, use the
  @ref Rodin::Geometry::Sharder<Context::MPI> "Sharder" with an explicit
  partitioning step.  The workflow is:

  1. **Root rank**: generate a local mesh, compute cell-cell connectivity,
     partition with a @ref Rodin::Geometry::Partitioner "Partitioner", then
     `shard()` and `scatter()`.
  2. **All ranks**: `gather()` to receive the local shard as an MPI mesh.

  @code{.cpp}
  MPI::Sharder sharder(mpi);

  if (world.rank() == 0)
  {
    Geometry::LocalMesh mesh;
    mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Tetrahedron, {32, 32, 32});
    const size_t cellDim = mesh.getDimension();
    mesh.getConnectivity().compute(cellDim, cellDim);

    Geometry::BalancedCompactPartitioner partitioner(mesh);
    partitioner.partition(world.size());

    sharder.shard(partitioner);
    sharder.scatter(0);
  }

  auto mesh = sharder.gather(0);
  @endcode

  The `distribute()` convenience method combines `shard()`, `scatter()`, and
  `gather()` in one call:

  @code{.cpp}
  auto mesh = sharder.distribute(partitioner, 0);
  @endcode

  @section guides-mpi-shard Shard: Rank-Local View

  A @ref Rodin::Geometry::Shard "Shard" is a local mesh enriched with
  distributed metadata.  It classifies every polytope into one of three
  mutually exclusive states:

  | State | Meaning |
  |-------|---------|
  | **Owned** | Belongs to this partition and this rank is the designated owner |
  | **Shared** | Belongs to this partition but owned by another rank |
  | **Ghost** | Does not belong to this partition; imported as overlap |

  Key invariants:
  - **Owned ∪ Shared** = entities defining the local partition
  - Every distributed entity has exactly one owner across all ranks
  - Ghost entities provide stencil support and adjacency completeness

  The shard provides bidirectional index maps between local shard indices and
  distributed (global) indices:

  @code{.cpp}
  const auto& shard = mesh.getShard();

  // Local → distributed
  Index globalIdx = shard.getPolytopeMap(d).left[localIdx];

  // Distributed → local
  Index localIdx = shard.getPolytopeMap(d).right.at(globalIdx);

  // Ownership queries
  bool owned  = shard.isOwned(d, localIdx);
  bool shared = shard.isShared(d, localIdx);
  bool ghost  = shard.isGhost(d, localIdx);
  bool local  = shard.isLocal(d, localIdx);  // Owned || Shared
  @endcode

  @section guides-mpi-connectivity Connectivity and Reconciliation

  After receiving a shard, you must compute local connectivity for the
  incidence relations you need.  However, entities discovered independently
  on neighboring shards may not yet have consistent distributed indices or
  ownership.  The `reconcile()` method resolves this:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Compute face-to-cell incidence locally
  mesh.getConnectivity().compute(2, 3);

  // Reconcile faces across MPI ranks:
  // - identifies shared entities via their vertex keys
  // - assigns unique distributed indices
  // - determines a unique owner rank per entity
  // - updates shard metadata (ownership, owner/halo maps, index maps)
  mesh.reconcile(2);
  @endcode

  @subsection guides-mpi-reconcile-details How Reconciliation Works

  Reconciliation is an iterative peer-to-peer convergence algorithm:

  1. Each rank identifies candidate shared entities from the ghost layer and
     exchanges their vertex keys with face-adjacent neighbor ranks.
  2. Matching entities are identified; distributed IDs and ownership are
     propagated iteratively until global convergence (checked via MPI
     all-reduce).
  3. Communication is restricted to K face-adjacent neighbors (K is O(1)),
     making the algorithm weak-scalable.

  @subsection guides-mpi-reconcile-options ReconcileOptions

  The `reconcile()` method accepts an optional
  `ReconcileOptions` struct to control convergence:

  | Field | Default | Description |
  |-------|---------|-------------|
  | `maxOwnerRounds` | unlimited | Cap on owner-convergence rounds |
  | `maxGidRounds` | unlimited | Cap on distributed-ID-convergence rounds |
  | `globalCheckPeriod` | 1 | How often global convergence is checked |
  | `strictRoundCap` | false | If true, exceeding a cap throws |

  For codimension-1 entities (faces in 3D, edges in 2D), `maxOwnerRounds=1`
  typically suffices because each face is shared by at most 2 cells.  For
  lower-dimensional entities (edges in 3D), unbounded rounds are the safe
  default since the holder graph diameter depends on mesh topology.

  @section guides-mpi-queries Global Queries

  Global geometric queries on the distributed mesh automatically perform MPI
  reductions.  Each rank accumulates contributions from its **owned** entities,
  then the results are summed across ranks:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Total number of cells across all ranks
  size_t totalCells = mesh.getCellCount();

  // Total volume (each rank contributes owned cells)
  Real vol = mesh.getVolume();

  // Volume restricted to an attribute
  Real vol_attr = mesh.getVolume(2);   // attribute 2

  // Total perimeter (owned boundary faces only)
  Real perim = mesh.getPerimeter();
  @endcode

  @section guides-mpi-io I/O

  The MPI module provides HDF5-based I/O for distributed meshes.  Each rank
  saves or loads its own shard independently:

  @code{.cpp}
  // Rank-dependent filename
  mesh.save(
    [](int rank) { return "mesh." + std::to_string(rank) + ".h5"; },
    IO::FileFormat::HDF5);

  // Or save the shard directly (MEDIT format)
  mesh.getShard().save("Shard." + std::to_string(world.rank()) + ".mesh",
                       IO::FileFormat::MEDIT);
  @endcode

  For XDMF output (ParaView visualization), pass the MPI communicator:

  @code{.cpp}
  IO::XDMF xdmf(MPI_COMM_WORLD, "output/simulation");
  xdmf.setMesh(mesh);
  xdmf.add("u", u.getSolution());
  xdmf.write();
  xdmf.close();
  @endcode

  @section guides-mpi-fes Distributed Finite Element Spaces

  The P1 finite element space is specialized for MPI meshes.  It wraps a
  local shard P1 space and augments it with distributed global indexing:

  @code{.cpp}
  P1 Vh(mesh);  // mesh is Mesh<Context::MPI>
  @endcode

  Owned DOFs receive contiguous global ranges per rank, while ghost DOFs are
  synchronized from their owner ranks.  The space provides bidirectional
  local↔global DOF index maps.

  @section guides-mpi-example Complete Example

  This example solves the Poisson equation on a distributed mesh using MPI
  and PETSc:

  @code{.cpp}
  #include <Rodin/MPI.h>
  #include <Rodin/PETSc.h>
  #include <Rodin/Geometry.h>
  #include <Rodin/Variational.h>
  #include <Rodin/IO/XDMF.h>

  namespace mpi = boost::mpi;
  using namespace Rodin;
  using namespace Rodin::Geometry;
  using namespace Rodin::Variational;

  int main(int argc, char** argv)
  {
    mpi::environment env(argc, argv);
    mpi::communicator world;
    Context::MPI mpi(env, world);
    PetscInitialize(&argc, &argv, PETSC_NULLPTR, PETSC_NULLPTR);

    // Partition and distribute mesh from root
    MPI::Sharder sharder(mpi);
    if (world.rank() == 0)
    {
      LocalMesh mesh;
      mesh = mesh.UniformGrid(Polytope::Type::Tetrahedron, {16, 16, 16});
      mesh.scale(1.0 / 15);
      const size_t cellDim = mesh.getDimension();
      mesh.getConnectivity().compute(cellDim, cellDim);

      BalancedCompactPartitioner partitioner(mesh);
      partitioner.partition(world.size());
      sharder.shard(partitioner);
      sharder.scatter(0);
    }
    auto mesh = sharder.gather(0);

    // Compute distributed connectivity
    mesh.getConnectivity().compute(2, 3);
    mesh.reconcile(2);

    // Build FE space and define problem
    P1 Vh(mesh);
    RealFunction f = 1;

    PETSc::Variational::TrialFunction u(Vh);
    PETSc::Variational::TestFunction  v(Vh);

    Problem poisson(u, v);
    poisson = Integral(Grad(u), Grad(v))
            - Integral(f, v)
            + DirichletBC(u, Zero());

    Solver::CG(poisson).solve();

    // Write XDMF output for ParaView
    IO::XDMF xdmf(MPI_COMM_WORLD, "Poisson");
    xdmf.setMesh(mesh);
    xdmf.add("u", u.getSolution());
    xdmf.write();
    xdmf.close();

    PetscFinalize();
  }
  @endcode

